// DataOps_MLOps_Flow — Integrated Service Architecture (Target, No Tools)
// Manual render: https://app.eraser.io > Diagram-as-code (cloud architecture).
//   Create a NEW canvas named "DataOps_MLOps_Flow" (canvas name = file name;
//   the cloud architecture syntax has no title line, so the title is set
//   via the canvas name). Do not overwrite an existing canvas with another title.
// Export the PNG to materials/reports/figures/DataOps_MLOps_Flow.png
// Reference: Figure IV.1 (fig:integrate-service), placed in Section IV.1
//   (sec:layanan-industri) after the classification of the nine platform layers and
//   before tool selection in Section IV.2 (sec:pemilihan-tools).
// SCOPE: INTER-LAYER RELATIONSHIPS only. The nine layers are drawn flat as
//   single nodes, without plane group boxes and without component detail
//   inside them, so the canvas stays readable and the PNG font stays large. Component
//   and tool detail for each layer is already drawn in the ten
//   Layer_*.eraser files (Section IV.3, sec:arsitektur-terintegrasi).
// The no-tools rule still holds: no tool names and no
//   platform/components/ component at all, so the integration target reads before
//   tool selection is made.
// CONNECTIVITY: every node must have at least one edge. Only
//   directed > and bidirectional <> arrows are used. No dashed lines and
//   no legend nodes without an edge. Target maturity markers (MLOps level
//   2 googlecloud2024mlops and the DataOps stage munappy2020adhoc, Section II.1.2
//   subsec:maturity) are stated in the Section IV.1 prose, not as nodes.
// Icons: EVERY node carries a generic icon, not a product logo, since no tools.
//   Icon names verified against docs.eraser.io/docs/icons.
// Rendering MUST be horizontal (direction right). Included without \rotatebox.
// Layer name source: Table layanan_industri (tab:layanan_industri). The direction of each
//   edge follows the stage order in its sources (amershi2019software,
//   munappy2020adhoc, kreuzberger2023mlops, googlecloud2024mlops,
//   najafabadi2024analysis) and the final implementation form in Chapter V.

direction right

// Actors and substrate
External Data Source [icon: cloud, color: gray, label: "External Data Source (API, files, databases)"]
Platform User [icon: users, color: gray, label: "Platform User (business, data, model)"]
Kubernetes Control Plane [icon: kubernetes, color: gray, label: "Kubernetes Control Plane (single, shared)"]

// Nine platform layers, flat without group boxes
Data Ingestion and Processing Layer [icon: download, color: orange]
Data Storage Layer [icon: database, color: green]
Feature Service Layer [icon: package, color: green]
Data Governance Layer [icon: book, color: teal]
Orchestration and Infrastructure Layer [icon: server, color: blue]
Model Lifecycle Layer [icon: refresh-cw, color: purple]
Observability Layer [icon: eye, color: teal]
GitOps and Continuous Delivery Layer [icon: git-branch, color: blue]
Platform Security Layer [icon: shield, color: red]

// Data integration artery toward the model
External Data Source > Data Ingestion and Processing Layer: external data intake
Data Ingestion and Processing Layer > Data Storage Layer: processed data
Data Storage Layer > Feature Service Layer: feature source
Feature Service Layer > Model Lifecycle Layer: training and inference features

// Governance collects cross-layer metadata and holds back defective data
Data Ingestion and Processing Layer <> Data Governance Layer: metadata and quality gate
Data Storage Layer > Data Governance Layer: data asset metadata
Feature Service Layer > Data Governance Layer: feature metadata
Model Lifecycle Layer > Data Governance Layer: model and experiment lineage

// Observability closes the retraining loop and supplies promotion metrics
Data Ingestion and Processing Layer > Observability Layer: data monitoring signal
Model Lifecycle Layer <> Observability Layer: model monitoring signal and retraining feedback
Observability Layer > GitOps and Continuous Delivery Layer: canary analysis metrics

// Orchestration and delivery run the workloads of every layer
Kubernetes Control Plane > Orchestration and Infrastructure Layer: runs workloads
Orchestration and Infrastructure Layer > Data Ingestion and Processing Layer: schedules data pipelines
Orchestration and Infrastructure Layer > Model Lifecycle Layer: runs ML pipelines
GitOps and Continuous Delivery Layer > Model Lifecycle Layer: model and service release
GitOps and Continuous Delivery Layer > Kubernetes Control Plane: declarative reconciliation

// Security gates user access to the layers used directly
Platform User > Platform Security Layer: access via API gateway
Platform Security Layer > Feature Service Layer: controlled feature API access
Platform Security Layer > Model Lifecycle Layer: controlled inference access
Platform Security Layer > Data Governance Layer: controlled catalog access
